# Data Sufficiency: Deciding With Less

> Data sufficiency flips the usual exam game: the answer itself is not the
> goal — deciding whether the given information is enough to reach it is.
> This lesson builds the option logic, the AD/BCE fork, and the discipline
> of deciding without solving.

[important]
Data sufficiency measures a decision, not a computation. Every question
asks one thing: does the information pin down a unique answer? You win
by classifying the data quickly, and you lose by solving for values the
options never ask for.
[/important]

## 1. What Data Sufficiency Tests

[definition]
**Data sufficiency (DS)** is a question format that presents a stem with an
unknown — a value, a yes/no fact, or a relationship — followed by two
statements, I and II. Your task is to judge whether the stem can be
answered *uniquely* using I alone, II alone, both, or neither.
[/definition]

Every DS question has three parts:

| Part | What it does | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Stem | states the unknown precisely | What is Ravi's present age? |
| Statement I | adds one piece of data | Ravi is 12 years older than Meena. |
| Statement II | adds a second piece | Five years ago Meena was half of Ravi's age. |

The skills being tested are narrow and trainable:

- reading the stem precisely — what exactly is unknown?
- translating each statement into constraints (equations, positions, relations)
- counting constraints against unknowns
- resisting the urge to compute the final value

[tip]
Say the stem as a question with a blank in it. "What is Ravi's age?" has
one blank. "Is Ravi older than Meena?" has a yes/no blank. The kind of
blank decides what *counts* as sufficient data.
[/tip]

## 2. The Two Option Families

Exams phrase the five possible verdicts in two standard ways. Learn both
by heart — misreading an option line is the cheapest way to lose a mark
in this topic.

| Verdict | Older / SSC-style wording | Banking / newer wording |
| --- | --- | --- |
| I alone works | If statement I alone is sufficient but II alone is not | Statement I alone is sufficient, II is not |
| II alone works | If statement II alone is sufficient but I alone is not | Statement II alone is sufficient, I is not |
| Either alone works | If either statement alone is sufficient | Either statement alone is sufficient |
| Both needed | If both statements together are needed | Both statements together are needed |
| Neither works | If neither statement is sufficient | Neither is sufficient even together |

Older papers keep four options and fold "neither" away; newer banking
papers often carry five options including "neither". Either way, the
*decision ladder* behind the options never changes.

[note]
The two statements are always true and never contradict each other in a
well-formed question. If your reading of I clashes with your reading of
II, re-read the stem before suspecting the paper.
[/note]

## 3. The AD/BCE Fork: Always Test Statement I First

The single biggest time-saver in DS is a fixed order of testing. Check
**statement I alone first**, then **statement II alone**, then combine.
This order creates the classic **AD/BCE fork**:

- If I alone is sufficient, the answer can only be **A** (I alone) or
  **D** (both needed) — II no longer matters for the verdict, only for
  the split between A and D.
- If I alone is *not* sufficient, the answer must be **B**, **C** or
  **E** — and D is eliminated instantly.

@diagram flowchart id="ds-decision-flow" layout="hierarchical" aria="Data sufficiency decision process from stem to final option"
node start label="Read the stem: know the exact blank" shape="pill"
node s1 label="Is I alone sufficient?" shape="diamond"
node s2 label="Is II alone sufficient?" shape="diamond"
node both label="Are I and II together sufficient?" shape="diamond"
node a label="Answer: I alone" shape="round-rect"
node b label="Answer: II alone" shape="round-rect"
node d label="Answer: both together" shape="round-rect"
node e label="Answer: neither" shape="round-rect"

start -> s1
s1 -> a
s1 -> s2
s2 -> b
s2 -> both
both -> d
both -> e
@/diagram

[definition]
The **AD/BCE fork** is the priority ladder of data sufficiency: test I
alone before II, and let the first result cut the option list roughly in
half before you look at the second statement.
[/definition]

Notice what the fork does *not* say. It never asks you to find the
actual answer — only whether each branch pins the stem down uniquely.

## 4. Sufficiency Is Not Solving

The most expensive habit in DS is solving completely. Consider:

**What is the value of x?**
- **I.** 3x + 2y = 18
- **II.** y is a positive integer

Statement I has one equation and two unknowns — structurally it cannot
pin x, no matter how pretty the numbers look. You can declare I
insufficient in about five seconds without computing anything. Statement
II says nothing about the link between x and y by itself. Together?
For *every* positive integer y there is an x = (18 − 2y)/3, and several
values work — so not even both suffice.

[warning]
The "solve everything" trap: candidates who begin by solving for x and y
burn two minutes per question and still misclassify. The exam pays for
the verdict — *sufficient or not* — never for the value.
[/warning]

The reverse also holds: when data *is* sufficient, you may never learn
the value. If I says x is the number of days in February in a leap year,
you can mark "I alone" and move on; the value 29 is irrelevant to the
option chosen.

## 5. The DS Topic Tree

DS questions pull their content from a short list of families. Knowing
the family tells you which *type* of check to run — equation counting
for arithmetic, uniqueness of arrangement for reasoning.

@diagram tree id="ds-family-tree" layout="tree" aria="Families of data sufficiency questions"
  "Data sufficiency" -> "Arithmetic DS"
  "Data sufficiency" -> "Reasoning DS"
  "Arithmetic DS" -> "Ages"
  "Arithmetic DS" -> "Percentages"
  "Arithmetic DS" -> "Ratio, profit-loss, TSD"
  "Arithmetic DS" -> "Simple equations"
  "Reasoning DS" -> "Seating and puzzles"
  "Reasoning DS" -> "Blood relations"
  "Reasoning DS" -> "Coding-decoding"
  "Reasoning DS" -> "Ordering and ranking"
@/diagram

Arithmetic DS asks "do the equations determine the numbers?" and is
covered in the next lesson. Reasoning DS asks "do the clues determine a
unique arrangement, code or relation?" and is covered in the third.

## 6. Reading the Stem: What Counts as an Answer

Sufficiency means *uniqueness*, and uniqueness depends on the kind of
blank the stem leaves.

| Stem type | Sufficient means | Example of a unique answer |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Value ("What is x?") | exactly one value possible | x = 63, never x = 63 or 36 |
| Yes/No ("Is x > 5?") | a definite yes or a definite no | "definitely no" is fully sufficient |
| Relation ("How are A and B related?") | one named relation | uncle, not "uncle or aunt" |
| Count ("How many are between them?") | one number | 6, never 6 or 8 |

[definition]
A statement is **sufficient** when the stem's answer is forced to a
single possibility; it is **insufficient** when at least two different
answers remain possible, even if you personally cannot construct them.
[/definition]

Two hidden assumptions appear again and again: ages move in whole years
unless stated otherwise, and "two-digit number" means the ten's digit is
at least 1. Good candidates spend their first five seconds on the stem
exactly because such phrases change the verdict.

## 7. Two Quick Walkthroughs

**Walkthrough 1 — a yes/no stem.** Is x greater than 5?
- **I.** x > 7
- **II.** x > 3

Statement I forces x past 7, so "yes" is certain — I alone is
sufficient. Statement II leaves x = 4 (no) and x = 6 (yes) both alive —
insufficient. The AD/BCE fork says: I worked, so the answer is I alone.
No value of x was ever needed.

**Walkthrough 2 — a value stem.** What is the two-digit number?
- **I.** The sum of its digits is 9.
- **II.** The ten's digit exceeds the unit's digit by 3.

I alone allows 18, 27, 36, 45, 54, 63, 72, 81, 90 — insufficient. II
alone allows 30, 41, 52, 63, 74, 85, 96 — insufficient. Together, only
63 appears in both lists — both statements are needed. Notice the
technique: we never derived the digits; we intersected two possibility
lists.

[example]
Exam method: **(1)** restate the stem as a blank, **(2)** test I alone
against the blank, **(3)** test II alone, **(4)** combine only if both
alone failed, **(5)** mark the option the ladder lands on.
[/example]

## 8. Traps, Timing and Exam Craft

A well-set DS paper spreads its answers across the options, so pattern
guessing ("both is safest") has no edge. A typical 40-question set looks
roughly like this:

@chart donut title="Typical answer spread in a 40-question DS set (illustrative)" labels:
I alone
II alone
Either alone
Both together
Neither

data:
10
8
9
11
2
@/chart

| Trap | What it looks like | Defence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| "I looks easier" bias | jumping to mark I-related options | judge each statement on constraints, not looks |
| Solving completely | deriving x, y, z before answering | ask "can two answers survive?" instead |
| Marking "both" too early | I failed, so you combine without checking II alone | statement II may finish the job by itself |
| Yes/no stems read wrongly | treating "definitely no" as insufficient | a forced answer of either polarity is sufficient |
| Hidden assumptions missed | ignoring "whole years", "distinct digits", "facing north" | re-read the stem before marking |

[tip]
Budget **30–45 seconds per DS question**. The decision ladder is short
by design: five seconds on the stem, fifteen on statement I, ten on
statement II, ten on combining or marking. If a statement needs a full
page of algebra to even assess, you are usually solving when you should
be counting.
[/tip]

[warning]
Never mark "both statements together" merely because each statement
alone failed. Two insufficient statements can still fail together — one
equation and one unrelated fact determine nothing, no matter how you
pair them.
[/warning]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Point | One-line recap |
| --- | --- |
| Core skill | decide if data pins a unique answer, not compute it |
| Priority ladder | I alone, then II alone, then combine |
| AD/BCE fork | I sufficient: A or D; I insufficient: B, C or E |
| Sufficient | exactly one answer survives the stem's blank |
| Yes/no stems | definite yes *or* definite no both count |
| Option families | older 4-option and newer 5-option wordings |
| Time budget | 30–45 seconds per question |
| Biggest trap | solving for values instead of counting possibilities |

[summary]
Data sufficiency is a classification game. Read the stem, test statement
I alone, test statement II alone, combine only when forced, and mark the
verdict the ladder produces. Sufficiency is about uniqueness of the
answer — never about the answer's value.
[/summary]
